<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.9.2: Writing an Equation to Represent a Constraint</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00205</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.9.2: Writing an Equation to Represent a Constraint</md:title>
    <md:uuid>f7fc97e9-6e1a-49d8-a2b3-b0b211982823</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">An automobile manufacturer is preparing a shipment of cars and trucks on a cargo ship that can carry 21,600 tons.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">The cars weigh 3.6 tons each, and the trucks weigh 7.5 tons each.</para>
      
        <media alt="A ship carrying cargo containers.">
          <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_11_03_01.jpg"/>
        </media>
      
      <!--Q1-->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">Write an equation that represents the weight constraint of a shipment. Let \( c \) be the number of cars and \( t \) be the number of trucks.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00005">\( 3.6c+7.5t=21,600 \)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <para id="para-00006">For numbers 2 - 5, use the following situation: For one shipment, trucks are loaded first and cars are loaded afterward. (Even though trucks are bulkier than cars, a shipment can consist of all trucks as long as it is within the weight limit.)</para>
      <exercise id="cdfd9a5c-09c6-496b-8513-a9644a629f57" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00007">Find the number of cars that can be shipped if the cargo already has 480 trucks.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00008">Correct! 5000</para>
          <para id="para-00009">Nice try! The correct answer is 5000.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="c7a64605-1755-4589-a66b-cbafa3668d84" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00010">Find the number of cars that can be shipped if the cargo already has 1500 trucks.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00011">Correct! 2875</para>
          <para id="para-00012">Nice try! The correct answer is 2875.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_9242f6f9-b898-46bf-8492-91431dba28a3" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00013">Find the number of cars that can be shipped if the cargo already has 2736 trucks.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00014">Correct! 300</para>
          <para id="para-00015">Nice try! The correct answer is 300.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Q5-->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00016">Find the number of cars that can be shipped if the cargo already has \(t\) trucks</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00017">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00018">\(\frac{21,600-7.5t}{3.6}\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <para id="para-00019">Use the following information for 6 - 8. For a different shipment, cars are loaded first, and then trucks are loaded afterward.</para>
      <!--Q6-->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00020">Write an equation you could enter into a calculator or a spreadsheet tool to find the number of trucks that can be shipped if the number of cars is known.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00021">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00022">\( t=\dfrac{21,\!600-3.6c}{7.5} \), where \( c \) is the number of cars.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <exercise id="ddd2a6db-692e-4c7e-892d-9596a9d59ab0" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00023">Use your equation and a calculator or a computer to find the number of trucks that can be shipped if the cargo already has 1000 cars.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00024">Correct! 2400</para>
          <para id="para-00025">Nice try! The correct answer is 2400.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_0eefd012-aa54-4abc-b55b-09f5e29a762a" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00008">
          <para id="para-00026">Use your equation and a calculator or a computer to find the number of trucks that can be shipped if the cargo already has 4250 cars.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00008">
          <para id="para-00027">Correct! 840</para>
          <para id="para-00028">Nice try! The correct answer is 840.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
    <!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you
      will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->
    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
      <section id="sect-00003">
        <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
        <para id="para-00029">
          <quote id="quote-00001">For numbers 1 and 2, use the following situation:For yet another shipment, the manufacturer is also shipping motorcycles, which weigh 0.3 ton each.</quote>
       </para>
        <!--Start
      interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all
      have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the
      button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
        <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00004">
          <problem id="prob-00009">
            <para id="para-00030">Write an equation that you could enter into a calculator or a spreadsheet tool to find the number of motorcycles that can be shipped, \( m \), if the number of cars and trucks are known.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00009">
            <para id="para-00031">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00032">\( m=\dfrac{21,\!600-7.5t - 3.6c}{0.3} \)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--End
      interaction-->
        <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00005">
          <problem id="prob-00010">
            <para id="para-00033">Use your equation to find the number of motorcycles that can be shipped if the cargo already contains 1,200 trucks and 3,000 cars.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00010">
            <para id="para-00034">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00035">6000</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--End
      interaction-->
      </section>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00004">
      <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
        <para id="para-00036">The total revenue a high school makes from a play is T dollars. The revenue is made from selling tickets at $3.25 per child ticket, c,  and $7.50 for each adult ticket, a. Which equation is best to find the number of child tickets sold?</para>
      </note>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00005">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      <section id="sect-00006">
        <title>Write Constraint Resources</title>
        <para id="para-00037">Review the following information from a previous lesson.</para>
        <section id="sect-00007">
          <title>Writing Equations Using Symbols</title>
          <para id="para-00038">When writing statements using algebraic symbols, the following steps may be helpful.</para>
          <para id="para-00039"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Read the problem.</para>
          <para id="para-00040"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Identify the variables and known values. If needed, sketch a picture of the scenario.</para>
          <para id="para-00041"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Write a sentence using the relationship among the values.</para>
          <para id="para-00042"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Translate the sentence into an equation.</para>
          <section id="sect-00008">
            <title>Example</title>
            <para id="para-00043">You are buying chicken and steak for a barbeque. Chicken costs $1.29/lb and steak costs $3.49/lb.</para>
          </section>
        </section>
        <!--Q1-->
        <exercise id="exer-00006">
          <problem id="prob-00011">
            <para id="para-00044">What variables might you need to use in this scenario?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00011">
            <para id="para-00045">Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here are some examples.</para>
            <list id="list-00001">
              <item>Let \(c\) represent the number of pounds of chicken that you can buy.</item>
              <item>Let \(s\) represent the number of pounds of steak that you can buy.</item>
              <item>Let \(T\) represent the total cost of the barbeque.</item>
            </list>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--Interaction
        End -->
        <!--Q2-->
        <exercise id="exer-00007">
          <problem id="prob-00012">
            <para id="para-00046">What sentence might you use to describe the total cost of the barbeque?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00012">
            <para id="para-00047">Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here are some examples.</para>
            <list id="list-00002">
              <item>The total cost of the barbeque is the sum of the total cost of all the chicken purchased and the total cost of all the steak purchased.</item>
              <item>The cost per pound of the chicken multiplied by the number of pounds purchased plus the cost per pound of the steak multiplied by the number of pounds purchased equals the total cost of the barbeque.</item>
            </list>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--Interaction
        End -->
        <!--Q3-->
        <exercise id="exer-00008">
          <problem id="prob-00013">
            <para id="para-00048">Write an equation to show the total cost, \(T\), of the meat.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00013">
            <para id="para-00049">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00050">\(T = 1.29c + 3.49s\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--Interaction
        End -->
        <!--Q4-->
        <exercise id="exer-00009">
          <problem id="prob-00014">
            <para id="para-00051">You have $100 to spend on the barbeque. Use this information and rewrite the equation \(T = 1.29c + 3.49s\) to find the number of pounds of chicken, \(c\), that you can buy.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00014">
            <para id="para-00052">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00053">\(c= \frac{100-3.49s}{1.29}\)</para>
            <para id="para-00054">\(T=1.29c+3.49s\) \(100=1.29c+3.49s\) \(100-3.49s=1.29c+3.49s-3.49s\) \(100-3.49s= 1.29c\) \(\frac{100-3.49s}{1.29}=\frac{ 1.29c}{1.29}\) \(\frac{100-3.49s}{1.29} =c\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--Interaction
        End -->
        <!--Q5-->
        <exercise id="exer-00010">
          <problem id="prob-00015">
            <para id="para-00055">You have $100 to spend on the barbeque. Use this information and rewrite the equation \(T = 1.29c + 3.49s\) to find the number of pounds of steak, \(s\), that you can buy.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00015">
            <para id="para-00056">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00057">\(s=\frac{ 100-1.29c}{3.49}\)</para>
            <para id="para-00058">\(T=1.29c+3.49s\) \(100=1.29c+3.49s\) \(100-1.29c=1.29c-1.29c+3.49s\) \(100-1.29c=3.49s\) \(\frac{100-1.29c}{3.49}=\frac{3.49s}{3.49}\) \(s= \frac{100-1.29c}{3.49}\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--Interaction
        End -->
        <note class="try" id="note-00002">
          <title>Try It: Write Constraint Equations</title>
          <!--Q#-->
        <exercise id="exer-00011">
            <problem id="prob-00016">
              <para id="para-00059">On a canoeing trip, you walk 4 mph then canoe at 7 mph and cover 30 miles. Write an equation to find how many miles you travel while canoeing.</para>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00016">
              <para id="para-00060">Compare your answer:</para>
              <para id="para-00061"><emphasis>STEP 1:</emphasis> Identify known variables. \(c\): miles canoeing \(w\): miles walked</para>
              <para id="para-00062"><emphasis>STEP 2:</emphasis> Write an equation to show the distance covered. 4w+7c=30</para>
              <para id="para-00063"><emphasis>STEP 3:</emphasis> Write an equation that solves for \(c\) the number of miles traveled while canoeing. \(c=\frac{30-4}{w7}\)</para>
            </solution>
          </exercise>
        </note>
        <!--Interaction
        End -->
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>